{
262: {skill_tested: "Identify a spatial insight supplied by reconstruction.", question: "According to the passage, what can the height of a reconstructed beam help determine?", option1: "The exact name of every worker who used the building", option2: "Whether the foundation stones were original", option3: "The date of every later repair", option4: "Whether a long object could have been turned inside the room", correctAnswer: "Whether a long object could have been turned inside the room", explanation: "Step 1: Supporters treat bodily movement through space as evidence.<br><br>Step 2: Beam height constrains whether a long object can be maneuvered indoors.<br><br>Answer: Whether a long object could have been turned inside the room", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
263: {skill_tested: "Identify a stated risk of visual completeness.", question: "Skeptics are most concerned that a complete-looking reconstruction may:", option1: "make uncertain choices appear historically certain.", option2: "prevent visitors from experiencing space.", option3: "preserve too much original fabric.", option4: "show multiple interpretations at once.", correctAnswer: "make uncertain choices appear historically certain.", explanation: "Step 1: Evidence may establish some features while leaving others unknown.<br><br>Step 2: Finished walls can make selected possibilities look inevitable.<br><br>Answer: make uncertain choices appear historically certain.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Author's Purpose and Perspective", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
264: {skill_tested: "Identify the author's central position.", question: "Which statement best expresses the passage's central idea?", option1: "Historic buildings should be reconstructed only when every detail is certain.", option2: "Reconstruction can test and communicate spatial claims when evidence, conjecture, modern needs, and later changes remain distinguishable.", option3: "Ruins provide no useful evidence about the past.", option4: "Modern safety requirements should always be disguised as historical features.", correctAnswer: "Reconstruction can test and communicate spatial claims when evidence, conjecture, modern needs, and later changes remain distinguishable.", explanation: "Step 1: Rebuilding can reveal movement, light, heat, and structural plausibility.<br><br>Step 2: The passage repeatedly requires visible uncertainty and documentation.<br><br>Answer: Reconstruction can test and communicate spatial claims when evidence, conjecture, modern needs, and later changes remain distinguishable.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
265: {skill_tested: "Analyze a design response to competing obligations.", question: "Using compatible but visibly dated modern supports primarily allows a project to:", option1: "avoid all changes required for public access.", option2: "make every visitor believe the supports are ancient.", option3: "meet current needs without presenting new construction as original fabric.", option4: "remove the need to preserve original material.", correctAnswer: "meet current needs without presenting new construction as original fabric.", explanation: "Step 1: Sites must satisfy safety and access requirements.<br><br>Step 2: Visible dates distinguish modern supports while compatible color limits distraction.<br><br>Step 3: The design accommodates use and maintains historical honesty simultaneously.<br><br>Answer: meet current needs without presenting new construction as original fabric.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
266: {skill_tested: "Infer why partial reconstruction can be informative.", question: "Temporary posts and projected alternate windows are valuable chiefly because they:", option1: "prove the precise roof and window arrangement.", option2: "eliminate the need for visitors to recognize uncertainty.", option3: "protect every original wall from weather.", option4: "represent spatial possibilities without requiring one uncertain version to appear permanent.", correctAnswer: "represent spatial possibilities without requiring one uncertain version to appear permanent.", explanation: "Step 1: Evidence may support more than one arrangement.<br><br>Step 2: Temporary or projected elements show volume and alternatives without full material commitment.<br><br>Step 3: Visitors can encounter uncertainty as part of the interpretation.<br><br>Answer: represent spatial possibilities without requiring one uncertain version to appear permanent.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
267: {skill_tested: "Evaluate what experimental reconstruction can establish.", question: "A successful trial joint provides evidence that:", option1: "the tested method can work under the selected conditions, though historical use remains unproven.", option2: "historical builders certainly used that joint in every structure.", option3: "machine-cut and hand-split boards behave identically.", option4: "written records are unnecessary whenever a joint holds.", correctAnswer: "the tested method can work under the selected conditions, though historical use remains unproven.", explanation: "Step 1: Building tests physical feasibility with particular wood, tools, and skill.<br><br>Step 2: Several feasible methods may exist, and records may not identify the historical choice.<br><br>Step 3: Experimental success establishes possibility, not exclusive historical fact.<br><br>Answer: the tested method can work under the selected conditions, though historical use remains unproven.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
268: {skill_tested: "Analyze why continuing documentation matters.", question: "Why should repairs to a reconstruction be recorded after it opens?", option1: "So all replacement pieces can be described as original", option2: "So future researchers can separate represented historical features from the reconstruction's own aging and maintenance", option3: "So visitors will stop polishing thresholds", option4: "So the building never changes in appearance", correctAnswer: "So future researchers can separate represented historical features from the reconstruction's own aging and maintenance", explanation: "Step 1: New materials weather, wear, and receive repairs over time.<br><br>Step 2: Those later changes can make the reconstruction appear more historically authoritative.<br><br>Step 3: Documentation preserves the chronology of the representation itself.<br><br>Answer: So future researchers can separate represented historical features from the reconstruction's own aging and maintenance", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
269: {skill_tested: "Analyze how a design makes scholarly disagreement accessible.", question: "What is the primary effect of constructing two different roof bays at the mill site?", option1: "It proves that both roofs existed simultaneously in the historical mill.", option2: "It conceals the fact that an early report proposed an alternative.", option3: "It turns competing interpretations into spatial experiences visitors can directly compare.", option4: "It restores the illusion of one seamless historical year.", correctAnswer: "It turns competing interpretations into spatial experiences visitors can directly compare.", explanation: "Step 1: Each bay embodies a different evidence-based proposal.<br><br>Step 2: Their adjacent placement reveals how each changes the room below.<br><br>Step 3: The visitor encounters the disagreement physically rather than receiving one choice as unquestioned fact.<br><br>Answer: It turns competing interpretations into spatial experiences visitors can directly compare.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
270: {skill_tested: "Synthesize the author's concept of reconstruction as a claim.", question: "Calling a reconstructed space a claim that can be inspected most strongly emphasizes that the reconstruction:", option1: "is authentic only when visitors cannot distinguish old and new material.", option2: "must provide a single final answer to every historical question.", option3: "has value only if its conjectural features are hidden.", option4: "embodies an evidence-based interpretation whose assumptions and limits should remain open to examination.", correctAnswer: "embodies an evidence-based interpretation whose assumptions and limits should remain open to examination.", explanation: "Step 1: Reconstruction translates documents, remains, comparisons, and experiments into physical form.<br><br>Step 2: That translation includes choices and uncertainty.<br><br>Step 3: Distinguishing evidence, conjecture, and later alteration lets visitors and researchers evaluate rather than merely accept the proposal.<br><br>Answer: embodies an evidence-based interpretation whose assumptions and limits should remain open to examination.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"}
}
